The better your internal platform works, the less anyone notices. There's no meeting agenda item for "nothing broke today." No applause for the nine-step workflow that became three. The value of good platform maintenance lives in pain that never happens — which makes it nearly impossible to explain to leadership who only see the cost line.

This is how I approach it.

> tl;dr
>
> - Measure actual time saved before and after, not just technical improvements
> - Express the saving in editorial capacity, not efficiency percentages
> - The before/after steps list is your best tool — steps removed are universally understood
> - Lead with the human metric in every communication; leave technical details for whoever asks

## Find the friction, then count it

The most useful thing I can do before a platform improvement ships is document the current pain precisely. Not "editors find the image workflow cumbersome" — but "creating a PDF cover image currently requires nine discrete steps, roughly 8 minutes per document."

That precision is what makes the after meaningful. When I replaced UNDRR's nine-step PDF cover workflow with an automated three-step process, I could calculate the saving per editor per month across the full team: 58 hours — roughly 1.5 work weeks — returned to editorial work every month.

The number matters less than the translation: this is **capacity**, not cost reduction. Leadership understood "58 hours back to the editorial team" immediately. "We optimized the ImageMagick pipeline" landed with nobody.

## The email that worked

Here's a condensed version of what I sent after the release:

> **Subject:** Latest platform release reclaims 1.5 work weeks per month for editors
>
> The recent platform release reduced friction for editors in measurable ways.
>
> The headline: these changes reclaim an estimated **58 hours per month** — 1.5 work weeks the editorial team can reinvest in content work instead of manual image processing.
>
> **Key example: document processing**
>
> **Before: 9 steps** (inconsistent, error-prone)
> 1. Create entry for PDF
> 2. Open Photoshop
> 3. Crop PDF cover
> 4. Save file
> 5. Create media entry for PDF cover
> 6. Upload image of PDF cover
> 7. Name entry
> 8. Specify 6 different image crops
> 9. Select image for PDF cover
>
> **After: 3 steps**
> 1. Create entry for PDF
> 2. System automatically generates PDF cover image
> 3. Select image, overriding if desired

Steps removed are universally understood. You don't need to explain ImageMagick or module architecture. Showing that something which took nine actions now takes three does the work.

## What makes this repeatable

The pattern holds across releases because it stays concrete at every step.

**Measure the friction before you fix it.** Estimate minutes per task, tasks per week, editors affected. "Significant time sink" is useless. "8 minutes × 3 PDFs/day × 48 editors" gives you something to calculate from.

**Express the saving in capacity, not efficiency.** "20% faster" is abstract. "Equivalent to 1.5 extra work weeks per month" lands with someone managing headcount.

**Lead with the human metric, leave technical details for whoever asks.** The subject line is for the saving. The technical specifics belong at the end — or in a separate message to the people who want them.

The work doesn't get more visible by itself. Making it visible is a deliberate, recurring practice — not a one-time announcement.

---

*The Drupal specifics: replaced a query-intensive contributed admin menu with a lean custom one; added a module using ImageMagick to auto-generate PDF cover images on upload; switched from `image_widget_crop` to `focal_point` for simplified image handling. Full impact story in [Reclaiming three weeks of editorial time every month](/work/2025/impact-story-editorial-efficiency/).*

